## Pros and Cons: Men's Cushion Cut Ring

### Pros

- **Cushion cut is 41% cheaper than round at the same carat.** A [1ct GIA G-VS1 cushion is $1,891](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/23740863?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). A 1ct GIA round starts at $3,230. Same carat, same certification, real savings. No other stone shape delivers this price gap at natural certification.
- **No corner chip risk.** Cushion corners are rounded. Standard prongs grip them naturally. You don't need to specify V-prong heads. You don't need annual corner checks. It's a low-maintenance stone for daily wear.
- **Heritage silhouette.** The cushion shape dates to the 19th century. On a wide men's band, it reads as heirloom, deliberate, and historically grounded. No other cut gives you this aesthetic at this price.
- **Same face-up footprint as princess at lower cost.** A 1ct cushion (5.5mm × 5.5mm) covers 79% of a 7mm band. That's the same as a 1ct princess. But cushion starts $250 less per carat and carries zero corner risk.
- **Lab-grown cushion reaches 1.5ct for $1,422.** The [IGI E-VVS2 1.5ct lab cushion at $1,422](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28961887?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is a 6.3mm × 6.3mm certified stone. Natural equivalent would start at $6,000+.

### Cons

- **Cushion cut has a soft glow, not sharp sparkle.** Cushion modified brilliant facets scatter light broadly rather than projecting sharp flashes. It looks warm and deep. If you want the high-contrast sparkle of a round brilliant, cushion won't deliver that in the same way.
- **L/W ratio variance.** Cushion cut is available in square (1:1) and elongated (up to 1:1.20) formats. For men's rings, square is the correct choice. An elongated cushion on a 7mm band looks off-axis. Check the stone dimensions, not just the carat weight.
- **Modified vs standard cushion.** Most certified cushion inventory is "cushion modified brilliant" — the facet pattern that gives maximum brightness. Standard cushion has a cross-shaped facet pattern — you might prefer it if you want a deeper antique look. Both are correct; "modified" is the more common and brighter option.
- **18k settings cost more for a softer metal.** The [18k channel ring at $2,832](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/mens-round-diamond-channel-engagement-ring-in-18k-white-gold-1-3-ct-tw-item-147395?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is $744 more than the 14k version. 18k is softer. Skip all 18k across this category.

